Two more things they ask about:
- `watch(source, cb, { immediate: true })` calls the callback right away with the current value, then on changes.
- `watch(source, cb, { flush: 'sync' })` calls it synchronously on **every** change: in the first example it would print `watch 1` and `watch 2` right away. `flush: 'post'` runs after the DOM update, when you need to measure elements.

Where it matters in real code: reading the size or scroll of an element right after changing data gives old values, measure after `nextTick` (or in a `post` watcher). And don't put side effects in `computed`: it may not run at all if nobody reads it.

```jsx
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

function Search() {
  // what the user typed
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  // what the server found
  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);
  // the loader
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    const q = query.trim();
    // empty input: clear the list, no request
    if (!q) {
      setResults([]);
      setLoading(false);
      return;
    }
    // a controller for this request
    const controller = new AbortController();
    // debounce: the request starts 300 ms after the last change
    const timer = setTimeout(async () => {
      setLoading(true);
      try {
        // encodeURIComponent: spaces and & won't break the URL
        const res = await fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(q)}`, {
          signal: controller.signal,
        });
        // fetch doesn't throw on 500
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
        setResults(await res.json());
      } catch (error) {
        // our own cancel isn't an error
        if (error.name !== 'AbortError') console.error(error);
      } finally {
        // only a request that wasn't cancelled turns the loader off
        if (!controller.signal.aborted) setLoading(false);
      }
    }, 300);
    // cleanup: runs before the next effect and on unmount
    return () => {
      // a new letter came earlier than 300 ms: the old timer is cancelled
      clearTimeout(timer);
      // the old request is cancelled, its response won't come
      controller.abort();
    };
  }, [query]);

  return (
    <>
      {/* a controlled input: the value comes from state */}
      <input value={query} onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)} type="search" />
      {loading && <p>Loading…</p>}
      <ul>
        {/* key by id, not by index */}
        {results.map((item) => <li key={item.id}>{item.title}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </>
  );
}
```

**Where it breaks and what to add in production:**
- No error and "nothing found" states.
- A cache of recent queries: going back to a previous query shouldn't refetch. In real projects this is TanStack Query: `useQuery({ queryKey: ['search', debouncedQuery], queryFn: ({ signal }) => ... })` with a `useDebouncedValue` hook gives the cache, cancellation and deduplication for free.
- A minimum query length, syncing with the URL (`?q=`).
- `useDeferredValue(query)` is an alternative for filtering a list that's already loaded: it doesn't reduce requests, it keeps typing smooth.
- In Strict Mode in development the effect runs twice on mount: our cleanup handles that correctly, which is a good sign.
- Accessibility: `aria-live` for the number of results, keyboard navigation through the list.

```jsx
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

function useFetch(url) {
  // the whole state in one object, so it's updated together
  const [state, setState] = useState({ data: null, error: null, loading: Boolean(url) });

  useEffect(() => {
    // no URL yet (e.g. the id isn't known): don't request
    if (!url) return;
    // this request's controller
    const controller = new AbortController();
    // start loading, keep the old data on screen meanwhile
    setState((prev) => ({ ...prev, error: null, loading: true }));

    fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal })
      .then((res) => {
        // fetch doesn't throw on 4xx/5xx: do it ourselves
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
        return res.json();
      })
      // success: data in, loader off
      .then((data) => setState({ data, error: null, loading: false }))
      .catch((error) => {
        // a cancelled request: the component moved on, do nothing
        if (error.name === 'AbortError') return;
        setState({ data: null, error, loading: false });
      });

    // cleanup: a new URL or unmount cancels this request
    return () => controller.abort();
  }, [url]);

  return state;
}

// usage
function Profile({ userId }) {
  const { data: user, loading, error } = useFetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);
  if (loading) return <p>Loading…</p>;
  if (error) return <p>Error: {error.message}</p>;
  return <h1>{user?.name}</h1>;
}
```

**Where it breaks and what to add in production:**
- No cache and no deduplication: two components with the same URL make two requests. That's why in real code it's TanStack Query (`useQuery`) or SWR: cache, background refetch, retries, invalidation after mutations.
- No `refetch` for a "Try again" button.
- Types: `useFetch<User>(url)`.
- Passing an options object (`useFetch(url, { headers })`) as a dependency starts a request on every render, because it's a new object each time. Keep the dependencies primitive or memoize them.
- `res.json()` on 204 No Content throws.
- In React 19 with a framework that supports it, data can come from server components or `use(promise)` with Suspense instead of an effect.

```jsx
import { useEffect, useLayoutEffect, useState } from 'react';

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  // runs on every render
  console.log('render', count);

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    // after the DOM update, before the paint
    console.log('layout', count);
  });

  useEffect(() => {
    // after the paint
    console.log('effect', count);
    // the cleanup runs before the next effect, with the OLD count
    return () => console.log('cleanup', count);
  }, [count]);

  function click() {
    // count is 0 in this render: both lines set 0 + 1
    setCount(count + 1);
    setCount(count + 1);
    // the variable didn't change, the new value is in the next render
    console.log('click', count);
  }

  return <button onClick={click}>{count}</button>;
}

// mount:
// render 0
// layout 0
// effect 0

// click:
// click 0
// render 1        ← one render for both setCount (batching), and 1, not 2
// layout 1
// cleanup 0
// effect 1
