What else they ask:
- **Strict Mode** in development renders twice and runs effects as "mount → cleanup → mount", so on mount you'd see `render 0` twice and `effect 0`, `cleanup 0`, `effect 0`. In production that doesn't happen. It's there to catch effects without proper cleanup.
- A child's effects run **before** the parent's: React goes bottom-up.
- Since React 18 batching works everywhere, including inside `setTimeout` and promises, not only in event handlers.

Where it matters in real code: "I called setState and logged the old value", sending a form with stale state right after `setState`, effects without cleanup that subscribe twice.

```js
function isValid(str) {
  // closing bracket → the opening one it needs
  const pairs = { ')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{' };
  // opening brackets that are waiting to be closed
  const stack = [];

  for (const ch of str) {
    if (ch === '(' || ch === '[' || ch === '{') {
      // an opening bracket: put it on the stack
      stack.push(ch);
    } else if (ch in pairs) {
      // a closing bracket: the top of the stack must be its pair
      // (pop on an empty stack gives undefined, which doesn't match either)
      if (stack.pop() !== pairs[ch]) return false;
    }
    // any other character is ignored
  }
  // everything opened must be closed
  return stack.length === 0;
}

// checks
isValid('({[]})');    // true
isValid('(]');        // false, the wrong pair
isValid('(()');       // false, one is left open
isValid(')(');        // false, closing before opening
isValid('a(b)c');     // true, letters are ignored
```

Why a stack: the bracket opened **last** must be closed **first**, which is exactly "last in, first out".

**Edge cases and follow-ups:**
- An empty string is valid. A string of only closing brackets fails on the first one.
- An early exit: if the length of a brackets-only string is odd, it can't be valid.
- Follow-up "only round brackets": a stack isn't needed, a counter is enough (+1 for `(`, −1 for `)`, never below zero, zero at the end).
- Follow-up "brackets inside quotes don't count": skip characters while inside a string.
- In real life the same idea is in parsers and linters: matching tags in HTML, JSX, template syntax.

```js
function isAnagram(a, b) {
  // different lengths can't be anagrams
  if (a.length !== b.length) return false;
  // letter → how many times it appears in a
  const count = {};
  for (const ch of a) {
    count[ch] = (count[ch] || 0) + 1;
  }
  for (const ch of b) {
    // b has a letter that a doesn't have, or has it more times
    if (!count[ch]) return false;
    // use one of these letters up
    count[ch] -= 1;
  }
  // lengths are equal and nothing went below zero, so the counts match
  return true;
}

function groupAnagrams(words) {
  // sorted letters → the words with these letters
  const groups = new Map();
  for (const word of words) {
    // 'tea' → 'aet': the same key for all anagrams
    const key = [...word].sort().join('');
    // first word with this key: start a group
    if (!groups.has(key)) groups.set(key, []);
    // add the word to its group
    groups.get(key).push(word);
  }
  // only the groups are needed, not the keys
  return [...groups.values()];
}

// checks
isAnagram('listen', 'silent');   // true
isAnagram('rat', 'car');         // false
groupAnagrams(['eat', 'tea', 'tan', 'ate', 'nat', 'bat']);
// [['eat', 'tea', 'ate'], ['tan', 'nat'], ['bat']]
```

The short version of the check, fine to say first: `[...a].sort().join('') === [...b].sort().join('')`. It's O(n log n) because of sorting; the counting version is O(n).

**Edge cases and follow-ups:**
- Case and spaces: `"Dormitory"` and `"dirty room"`. Ask, and normalize first: `str.toLowerCase().replace(/\s/g, '')`.
- `[...str]` splits by characters correctly (emoji and other characters made of two code units), `str.split('')` doesn't.
- Grouping complexity: O(n · k log k), where k is the word length. For long words the key can be built from letter counts instead of sorting.
- Unicode: `ё` and `е`, accented letters. `normalize('NFD')` if needed.

```js
function firstUnique(str) {
  // character → how many times it appears
  const count = new Map();
  // first pass: count everything
  for (const ch of str) {
    count.set(ch, (count.get(ch) || 0) + 1);
  }
  // second pass: in the original order, look for count 1
  for (const ch of str) {
    if (count.get(ch) === 1) return ch;
  }
  // every character repeats
  return null;
}

// checks
firstUnique('leetcode');       // 'l'
firstUnique('loveleetcode');   // 'v'
firstUnique('aabb');           // null
firstUnique('');               // null
```

Why two passes and not `str.indexOf(ch) === str.lastIndexOf(ch)` for each character: that's simple to write, but every check scans the string, so it's O(n²). Mention it as the naive solution and then improve it.

**Edge cases and follow-ups:**
- Return the index instead of the character: the second loop goes by index, `for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++)`.
- Case: are `A` and `a` the same character? Ask; if yes, lowercase first.
- `for...of` walks characters, not UTF-16 units, so emoji are counted correctly; indexes from `str[i]` would not be.
- A stream of characters (we can't read the string twice): keep the counts plus the insertion order of characters seen once; a `Map` keeps insertion order, so delete from it when a character repeats.

```js
// indexes of two numbers that add up to target (the classic task)
function twoSum(nums, target) {
  // number → its index, for the numbers we've already passed
  const seen = new Map();
  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    // how much is missing to the target
    const need = target - nums[i];
    // we've seen the missing number before: done
    if (seen.has(need)) return [seen.get(need), i];
    // remember the current one for the numbers after it
    seen.set(nums[i], i);
  }
  // no such pair
  return null;
}

// all unique pairs of values
function allPairs(nums, target) {
  // numbers we've already passed
  const seen = new Set();
  // 'a,b' → [a, b]; the key removes duplicate pairs
  const pairs = new Map();
  for (const n of nums) {
    const need = target - n;
    if (seen.has(need)) {
      // the smaller first, so [1, 4] and [4, 1] are the same pair
      const pair = [Math.min(n, need), Math.max(n, need)];
      pairs.set(pair.join(','), pair);
    }
    seen.add(n);
  }
  return [...pairs.values()];
}

// checks
twoSum([2, 7, 11, 15], 9);        // [0, 1]
twoSum([3, 3], 6);                // [0, 1]
allPairs([1, 4, 2, 3, 4, 1], 5);  // [[1, 4], [2, 3]]
```

Why "check, then remember" and not the other way around: otherwise for `target = 6` the number 3 would pair with itself.

**Edge cases and follow-ups:**
- **The array is sorted**: two pointers, one at the start and one at the end. Sum too small: move the left one right; too big: move the right one left. O(n) time and O(1) memory.
- Negative numbers and zero work without changes.
- "Three numbers that add up to zero" (3Sum): sort, fix one number, and run two pointers on the rest, O(n²).
- Ask what to return when there's no pair: `null`, `[]` or throw.

```js
function compress(str) {
  // the result is built piece by piece
  let result = '';
  // how many identical characters in a row so far
  let count = 1;

  for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++) {
    if (str[i] === str[i + 1]) {
      // the next one is the same: keep counting
      count++;
    } else {
      // the run ended: write the character and, if more than one, the number
      result += str[i] + (count > 1 ? count : '');
      // start counting the next run
      count = 1;
    }
  }
  return result;
}

// checks
compress('aaabb');      // 'a3b2'
compress('abc');        // 'abc'
compress('aabcccccaaa');// 'a2bc5a3'
compress('');           // ''
```

Why `str[i + 1]` is safe at the last character: it's `undefined`, which is never equal to a character, so the last run is always written.

The reverse task, unpacking, often follows:

```js
function decompress(str) {
  // a letter followed by an optional number: 'a3', 'b', 'c12'
  return str.replace(/(\D)(\d*)/g, (_, ch, n) => ch.repeat(Number(n || 1)));
}
decompress('a3b2c');   // 'aaabbc'
```

**Edge cases and follow-ups:**
- Digits in the input string (`"a111"`) make the compressed form ambiguous. Ask whether the input is letters only.
- "Return the original if the compressed one isn't shorter": compare lengths at the end.
- Building a string with `+=` in a loop is fine in modern engines. In other languages they expect an array and `join`.
- Case: `"aA"` is two different characters.

```js
function groupBy(items, key) {
  // group key → the objects of this group
  const groups = {};
  for (const item of items) {
    // the key can be a field name or a function: groupBy(users, (u) => u.age > 18)
    const groupKey = typeof key === 'function' ? key(item) : item[key];
    // first object of this group: create the array
    if (!groups[groupKey]) groups[groupKey] = [];
    // add the object to its group
    groups[groupKey].push(item);
  }
  return groups;
}

// checks
const users = [
  { name: 'Ann', role: 'admin' },
  { name: 'Bob', role: 'user' },
  { name: 'Kate', role: 'admin' },
];
groupBy(users, 'role');
// { admin: [Ann, Kate], user: [Bob] }
groupBy(users, (u) => u.name.length > 3 ? 'long' : 'short');
// { short: [Ann, Bob], long: [Kate] }

// the same with reduce, if they ask for a one-liner
const byRole = users.reduce((acc, u) => {
  (acc[u.role] ||= []).push(u);
  return acc;
}, {});
```

**Edge cases and follow-ups:**
- Keys of an object are always strings: `true` becomes `'true'`, `1` becomes `'1'`. If the original type matters, return a `Map`.
- A key like `__proto__` from user data breaks a plain object. `Object.create(null)` or a `Map` is safer.
- Built-in: `Object.groupBy(users, (u) => u.role)` (returns an object with a null prototype) and `Map.groupBy`.
- Follow-ups: count instead of collect (`countBy`), nested grouping (by role, then by city), or sort the groups by size.
